W5 in meld, extra W5 could be pair if I get another, but W5 is simple.  \n- W7,W8,W9: W7 and W8 are part of potential chow, but I already have W7,W8,W9 chow in hand? Actually, W7,W8,W9 is a chow, so I should keep them together.  \n- T4: I have T4 in meld, extra T4 could be pair if I get third T4 (but T4 only 1 left unshown? Unshown T4:1, so one T4 left in wall).  \n\nLooking at opponents' melds: next player has B2,B3,B4 chow and B7 pung; previous has B7,B8,B9 and T3,T4,T5. So many B and T tiles are used.  \n\nBest discard likely one that is safe (not likely to give opponent win) and improves my hand toward a pattern.  \n\nSafety: check opponents' discards:  \nNext player discarded W6, F4, J2, F1, W8, ... recent: T6.  \nAcross: discarded J1, B9, T5, J1, T1, T7, J3, B1, T1, T3, T3, T7, F1.  \nPrevious: discarded T2, B5, B5, T8, J1, B5, B1, W3, F3, F2, W8, T3, F4.  \n\nTiles that seem safe: maybe F2? Previous discarded F2, so possibly safe. B2? Not discarded much.  \n\nBut I need to improve hand. My hand has potential for Full Flush in W? I have W5,W7,W8,W9 and meld W3,W4,W5. If I aim for Full Flush (all W), I need to get rid of T4,T7,B2,F2. That's ambitious given tile count.  \n\nMaybe aim for Half Flush (W + honors). Then I need to discard B2 and T tiles.  \n\nGiven I have two chows already (T and W), maybe aim for Mixed Straight? I have W3,W4,W5 and T2,T3,T4, so if I get a chow in B for 7,8,9 or 1,2,3 etc. But I have B2 only.  \n\nConsidering tile availability: W tiles: many available. T tiles: T3 depleted, T4 1 left, T7 2 left, etc.  \n\nI think keep W7,W8,W9 chow, try to form another chow or pair. Discard F2 first as it's honor and not scoring pattern currently. Then hope to draw useful tiles.  \n\nReason: F2 is not part of any immediate pattern, and discarding it reduces honor count, possibly helping All Simples later. It's relatively safe as previous player discarded F2. Keeping W tiles maintains possibility of W-based hand.  \n\nAnswer: Play F2"}
